  "rationale": "Direct-match models for the target EFO term “ovarian neoplasm” are present (multiple ‘Malignant neoplasm of ovary’ scores, e.g., PGS000544–PGS000564). Among these exact-label candidates, PGS000549 has the strongest visible discrimination in its evaluation record (full-model AUROC 0.567 with European evaluation) and comparatively strong per-SD effect size (OR 1.263, 95% CI 1.15–1.387) and pseudo-R² (0.00996) versus the other ovarian-neoplasm-labeled siblings shown, which are mostly lower-AUROC (~0.52–0.56) and/or similar ORs. However, confidence is limited because these exact-label candidates come from the same repository-style publication family with modest performance overall, the reported AUROC appears to be a full-model metric (not explicitly PRS-only), and there are strong ovarian-cancer (ovarian carcinoma) candidates in the broader direct set with substantially higher reported AUROC (e.g., PGS003385 AUROC 0.717; PGS000082/PGS000793 ~0.656–0.66) but not the exact ‘ovarian neoplasm’ label—creating a phenotype-alignment vs. performance tradeoff for this target."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005220","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for sleep apnea, including multiple generic “Sleep apnea” models (PGS003204–PGS003218) and multiple “Obstructive sleep apnea” models (PGS003479, PGS003857, PGS003858, PGS005219, PGS005220). Domain knowledge indicates obstructive sleep apnea is the clinically dominant form of sleep apnea, making OSA models acceptable direct matches.

Among OSA candidates, PGS005220 is best supported by the visible evidence: it is disease-focused (OSA), uses a multi-score aggregation approach (“weighted PRSsummation PRS-CSs”) with a large variant set (~984k), is developed across multiple cohorts (FinnGen, MGBB, MVP), and reports strong discriminative effect size (OR=1.53 per SD; 95% CI 1.39–1.68) alongside a reported AUROC of 0.79 (noting this is a full-model AUROC). Compared with PGS003857/PGS003858, which use only genome-wide significant SNPs (6–18 variants) and report AUROC=0.77, PGS005220 has a stronger structural PRS construction and comparable/higher reported discrimination.

Primary limitation: across all candidates, PRS-comparable metrics (PGS-only AUROC or PGS-only R²) are not provided, and several reported high AUROCs are full-model values with covariates including BMI (and in some models additional adjustments), so the incremental PRS contribution cannot be isolated from the provided metadata. This prevents a high-confidence “best” selection despite PGS005220 being the most coherent direct-match package in the current context."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS003381","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates for uterine cancer are present, including (i) uterine/uterus-labeled models (PGS000541, PGS000542, PGS001795, PGS003740) and (ii) the clinically dominant subtype endometrial/uterine endometrial carcinoma models (e.g., PGS003381, plus several endometrial cancer models). Domain knowledge explicitly notes endometrial carcinoma is the dominant subtype of uterine carcinoma (~90%), so endometrial/uterine endometrial carcinoma scores remain direct matches for a uterine cancer target.

PGS003381 is preferred because it has strong endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: uterine endometrial carcinoma), uses a genome-wide method (lassosum; 529,365 variants) consistent with capturing polygenic signal better than sparse GWAS-hits/P+T options, and shows relatively strong reported full-model discrimination in a large European evaluation (AUROC 0.761; R² 0.11; n=144,479) with only basic covariates (age, top 20 PCs). 

Main limitation: across these candidates, the reported AUROC/R² are largely full-model and not explicitly PGS-only; therefore comparability of the performance metrics across models is limited, and a uterus-umbrella exact-label model (e.g., PGS001795) also exists but has weaker PRS-comparable evidence (reported pgs_only_r2 0.001948; AUROC 0.66) and comes from a broad framework-style global-biobank paper. This mix of endpoint labels and non-uniform PRS-only metrics keeps confidence below high."}
